    {
      "id": "c80c183a3caa",
      "title": "Wuthering Heights",
      "author": "Emily Brontë",
      "publicationDate": "1847-01-01",
      "pageCount": 300,
      "location": null,
      "lat": null,
      "lng": null,
      "status": "read",
      "notes": "Surprisingly did not want to put this down. I remembered struggling through pages in earlier life. But it is disturbing and I had some bad nights sleep with very animated nightmares while reading this. Most probably unrelated, but the darkness and wildness sits deeply within one while reading it. I am relieved it is over and happily. That I did not expect.\nI don’t think of this as a love story. It never made me feel anything sweet or even desirous - if that is a word. It cannot compare as a lover story to Pride and Prejudice or any other Austen. We are not witness to the apparent supposed development of love between C+H when they are kids, we only assume it must be through the third hand retelling of Nelly, during their playing together on the moors, and then it is suddenly declared by them each as adults to each other. It never felt personal or real or like love. More like possession, or some kind of childish and immature comfort brought about by shared trauma. Love, I never saw love in this story except for the love of Hindley for his daughter and perhaps Nelly over all her charges at some point. And possibly younger Cathy and Hareton at the end, although even that love seems to exist out of necessity and boredom and a lack of other options than as a love that I understand. Not a love story, but a great story, nonetheless.",
      "rating": 3,
      "dateRead": "2026-03-02",
      "genre": null,
      "medium": "book",
      "coverUrl": null,
      "quotes": [],
      "bookmarked": false,
      "createdAt": "2026-05-28T14:29:36.981634Z",
